.TH FSELECT 1
.SH NAME
fselect \- find files with SQL-like queries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fselect
.RB [ \-\-nocolor ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-errors ]
.RB [ \-\-us\-dates ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.PP
.B fselect
.RB [ top
.IR N ]
.I COLUMN
.RI [ ", " COLUMN " ..."]
.RB [ from
.IR ROOT
.RI [ ", " ROOT " ..."]]
.RB [ where
.IR EXPR ]
.RB [ group
.B by
.IR COLUMNS ]
.RB [ having
.IR EXPR ]
.RB [ order
.B by
.IR COLUMNS ]
.RB [ limit
.IR N ]
.RB [ offset
.IR N ]
.RB [ into
.IR FORMAT ]
.PP
.B fselect \-i
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fselect
is a command-line utility for finding files using an SQL-like query language.
The
.B fselect
command itself acts as the
.B select
keyword (i.e.,
.IR "file select" ).
An optional
.B select
keyword at the beginning of the query is accepted but not required.
.PP
Query columns may be file attributes, arbitrary string literals (quoted if they contain spaces),
function calls, or arithmetic expressions.
.PP
If
.B from
is omitted, the current directory is searched.
Multiple search roots may be listed, separated by commas.
Each root may have its own set of options (depth, symlinks, archives, etc.).
.PP
Queries containing
.BR > ,
.BR < ,
parentheses, or shell metacharacters such as
.BR * " and " ?
should be enclosed in double quotes to prevent shell interpretation.
.PP
Commas between column names are optional.
String literals do not need quotes unless they contain spaces or shell metacharacters.
Functions with no arguments do not require parentheses.
Curly braces may be used instead of parentheses.
Keywords, field names and function names are case-insensitive.
The clauses must appear in the order shown in the SYNOPSIS.
.SH QUERY CLAUSES
.TP
.BI top " N"
Return at most
.I N
rows; a synonym for
.BI limit " N"
written before the column list.
Giving both is an error.
.TP
.IR COLUMN ", ..."
Columns to output: fields, quoted or bare string literals, numbers, function calls and
arithmetic expressions over them.
.B *
expands to the standard columns (mode, user and group on Unix, then size, modification date and path).
A column may be named with
.BI "as " ALIAS \fR;
the alias may be used in the other columns of the same list (declared before or after it), in
.BR where ", " "group by" ", " having " and " "order by" ,
and inside correlated subqueries as
.IB ROOT_ALIAS . ALIAS \fR.
.TP
.BI from " ROOT [OPTIONS][, ROOT ...]"
Directories to search, each with optional search root options (see
.BR "SEARCH ROOTS" ).
A parenthesized subquery may stand in place of a directory (see
.BR SUBQUERIES ).
Several roots are searched one after another; the result is their union, not a join.
Without
.BR from ,
the current directory is searched.
.TP
.BI where " EXPR"
Condition each file must satisfy (see
.BR OPERATORS ).
A boolean field or function may stand alone as a condition, e.g.
.BR "where is_audio" .
Aggregate functions are not allowed here.
.TP
.BI "group by " COLUMN ", ..."
Collapse files into groups.
Grouping columns are fields, expressions, column aliases or positional numbers referring to the column list.
Without an aggregate in the column list the query returns one row per distinct group, like
.B "select distinct"
in SQL (the
.B distinct
keyword itself is not supported).
Every output column must be a grouping column, an expression over grouping columns, or an aggregate.
.TP
.BI having " EXPR"
Filter the groups after aggregation.
May use aggregate functions (including ones that are not selected), grouping columns and column aliases.
.B exists
subqueries are not supported here.
With aggregates but no
.BR "group by" ,
the whole result forms one group.
.TP
.BI "order by " COLUMN " [asc|desc], ..."
Sort the rows by fields, expressions, column aliases or positional numbers; descending with
.BR desc .
In a grouped query only selected columns, aggregates and grouping columns may be used.
.TP
.BI limit " N"
Return at most
.I N
rows;
.B "limit 0"
means no limit.
The form
.BI limit " OFFSET,N"
skips
.I OFFSET
rows first.
.TP
.BI offset " N"
Skip the first
.I N
rows.
.TP
.BI into " FORMAT"
Output format (see
.BR "OUTPUT FORMATS" );
must be the last clause.
.PP
Joins, unions and
.B distinct
are not supported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-interactive ", " \-i
Run in interactive mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Specify an alternative configuration file location.
.TP
.BR \-\-nocolor ", " \-\-no\-color
Disable colorized output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-errors
Suppress error messages.
.TP
.BR \-\-us\-dates
Interpret ambiguous dates in US format (MM/DD) instead of the default UK format (DD/MM).
.TP
.B \-\-everything
Take candidate paths from the Everything index (Windows; requires the
.B everything
build feature). See
.BR "INDEX-BACKED SEARCH" .
.TP
.B \-\-plocate
Take candidate paths from the plocate database (Linux; requires the
.B plocate
build feature). See
.BR "INDEX-BACKED SEARCH" .
.TP
.BR \-\-fields ", " \-\-functions ", " \-\-root\-options ", " \-\-output\-formats
Print the supported column names, function names, search root options or output formats and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show help and exit.
.SH COLUMNS AND FIELDS
.SS File naming
.TP
.B name
