Value of the named xattr.
.TP
.BR HAS_EXTATTR "(flag)"
True if the given
.BR lsattr (1)
flag is set.
Available only on Linux.
.TP
.BR HAS_ACL_ENTRY "(entry)"
True if the specified ACL entry exists.
Entry format:
.BR type : qualifier
(e.g.,
.BR user:john ,
.BR group:staff ).
.TP
.BR ACL_ENTRY "(entry)"
Permissions for the specified ACL entry.
.TP
.BR HAS_DEFAULT_ACL_ENTRY "(entry)"
True if the specified default ACL entry exists.
.TP
.BR DEFAULT_ACL_ENTRY "(entry)"
Permissions for the specified default ACL entry.
.TP
.BR HAS_CAPABILITY " / " HAS_CAP "(cap)"
True if the file has the given Linux capability.
.SS User functions
Available only on Unix when the
.B users
feature is enabled.
.TP
.BR CURRENT_UID ()
Current real UID.
.TP
.BR CURRENT_USER ()
Name of the current real UID.
.TP
.BR CURRENT_GID ()
Current primary GID.
.TP
.BR CURRENT_GROUP ()
Name of the current primary GID.
.SH FILE SIZE UNITS
Size comparisons and the
.B fsize
column understand the following unit suffixes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fBt\fR or \fBtib\fR    tebibyte    1024^4 bytes
\fBtb\fR          terabyte    1000^4 bytes
\fBg\fR or \fBgib\fR    gibibyte    1024^3 bytes
\fBgb\fR          gigabyte    1000^3 bytes
\fBm\fR or \fBmib\fR    mebibyte    1024^2 bytes
\fBmb\fR          megabyte    1000^2 bytes
\fBk\fR or \fBkib\fR    kibibyte    1024 bytes
\fBkb\fR          kilobyte    1000 bytes
.fi
.RE
.SH SEARCH ROOTS
Each search root may be followed by one or more options:
.TP
.BI mindepth " N"
Minimum search depth. Depth 1 skips the root directory itself and starts at its children.
.TP
.BI maxdepth " N"
Maximum search depth.
Depth 1 searches only the root directory.
Depth 2 also searches immediate subdirectories.
Synonym:
.BR depth .
.TP
.BR symlinks " / " sym
Follow symbolic links. Default is not to follow.
.TP
.BR archives " / " arc
Search inside archives (zip only). Default is not to search archives.
Archived entries report \fBpath\fR, \fBabspath\fR, \fBdir\fR and \fBabsdir\fR with the archive path as a prefix, e.g. pack.zip/sub/deep.txt.
They also report content fields such as
.BR line_count ", " word_count ", " encoding ", " contains() ", " mime ", " is_text " and " is_binary ,
as well as
.BR depth ,
and obey
.BR mindepth " / " maxdepth
counted from the search root.
.TP
.BR gitignore " / " git
Respect
.B .gitignore
files.
.TP
.BR hgignore " / " hg
Respect
.B .hgignore
files.
.TP
.BR dockerignore " / " docker " / " dock
Respect
.B .dockerignore
files.
.TP
.BR nogitignore " / " nogit
Disable
.B .gitignore
parsing.
.TP
.BR nohgignore " / " nohg
Disable
.B .hgignore
parsing.
.TP
.BR nodockerignore " / " nodocker " / " nodock
Disable
.B .dockerignore
parsing.
.TP
.B dfs
Depth-first traversal.
.TP
.B bfs
Breadth-first traversal (default).
.TP
.BR regexp " / " rx
Treat the root path as a regular expression matching against multiple directories.
.TP
.BI as " ALIAS"
Name the root so that its fields can be written as
.IB ALIAS . FIELD
in the query and referenced from correlated subqueries.
.PP
The
.BR gitignore ", " hgignore " and " dockerignore
defaults for every root can be set in the configuration file.
.SH INDEX-BACKED SEARCH
With
.B \-\-everything
(Windows, the Everything engine) or
.B \-\-plocate
(Linux, the plocate database) the candidate paths come from a prebuilt index instead of a directory walk,
which is much faster on large trees; the corresponding build feature must be enabled.
The same can be switched on with
.B "everything = true"
or
.B "plocate = true"
in the configuration file.
.PP
If the index is unavailable (Everything not running or its DLL missing, the plocate binary or
database missing), the search falls back to normal traversal.
.BR mindepth " and " maxdepth
are applied to the index results, and all fields, functions and clauses work as usual.
Roots with
.B archives
or any of the ignore-file options are always traversed normally.
Locations the index does not cover return no results.
.SH OPERATORS
.SS Comparison operators
.TP
.BR = " / " == " / " eq
Equal (with glob expansion and regular expression matching for strings).
.TP
.BR != " / " <> " / " ne
Not equal.
.TP
.BR === " / " eeq
Exact equal (no glob or regex expansion).
.TP
.BR !== " / " ene
Exact not-equal.
.TP
.BR > " / " gt
Greater than.
.TP
.BR >= " / " gte " / " ge
Greater than or equal.
.TP
.BR < " / " lt
Less than.
.TP
.BR <= " / " lte " / " le
Less than or equal.
.TP
.BR =~ " / " ~= " / " regexp " / " rx " / " rlike
Match regular expression.
.TP
.BR !=~ " / " !~= " / " notrx " / " notrlike
Does not match regular expression.
.TP
.B like
SQL LIKE pattern (
.B %
matches any sequence,
.B _
matches one character); matching is case-insensitive, and
.BR \e_ ", " \e% " and " \e\e
match the literal character.
.TP
.BR "not like" " / " notlike
Negation of
.BR like .
.TP
.B between
Range check:
.IB column " between " val1 " and " val2 \fR;
negated with
.BR "not between" " / " notbetween .
.TP
.B in
Set membership:
.IB column " in (" val1 ", " val2 ", ...)"
or
.IB column " in (subquery)" \fR,
where the subquery must select a single column; negated with
.BR "not in" " / " notin .
.TP
.B exists
Row existence test:
.B exists (subquery)
with any column list; negated with
.BR "not exists" " / " notexists .
.TP
.BR "is null" " / " "is not null"
True when the value is missing or empty, e.g.
.B width
of a file that is not an image.
.PP
Either side of a comparison may be a parenthesized subquery that selects a single column; its first row supplies the value (see
.BR SUBQUERIES ).
A missing numeric, date or boolean value is unknown rather than zero or false:
.BR = ", " != ", " in " and " "not in"
never match it.
.SS Arithmetic operators
.TP
.BR + " / " plus
Addition.
.TP
.BR - " / " minus
Subtraction.
.TP
.BR * " / " mul
Multiplication.
.TP
.BR / " / " div
Division.
.TP
.BR % " / " mod
Modulo.
.SS Logical operators
.TP
.B and
Logical conjunction.
.TP
.B or
Logical disjunction.
.TP
.B not
Logical negation. May appear before an operator or a condition.
.SH DATE AND TIME SPECIFIERS
When the
.B =
or
.B !=
operator is used with an inexact date, it is treated as an interval:
.PP
.RS
.TP
.B 2017-05-01
The entire day (00:00:00 to 23:59:59).
.TP
.B "2017-05-01 15"
One hour (15:00:00 to 15:59:59).
.TP
.B "2017-05-01 15:10"
One minute (15:10:00 to 15:10:59).
.RE
.PP
Other comparison operators accept more flexible date expressions:
.B today ,
.B yesterday ,
.B "last fri" ,
.B "apr 1" ,
.B 2017-05-01 ,
and numeric relative offsets (e.g.,
.B -2
means two days ago).
.PP
Dates default to UK locale (DD/MM). Use
.B \-\-us\-dates
or
.B us_dates = true
in the config file to switch to US format.
The safest representation is ISO 8601:
.BR "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" ,
with either a space or a
.B T
between the date and the time.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Rust-flavor regular expressions are used (see
.BR regex (7)
or the online documentation at https://docs.rs/regex).
.SH SUBQUERIES
A parenthesized query (curly braces work as well) may appear in these places:
.TP
In a comparison or in the column list
A scalar subquery must select a single column; its first row supplies the value, and no row yields a missing value:
.B "where size > (select max(size) from /backup)"
.TP
With in / not in
The subquery must select a single column.
.TP
With exists / not exists
Any column list; true when the subquery returns at least one row.
.TP
In the from clause
The rows of the inner query become the files the outer query examines.
A subselect takes root options and an alias like a directory does, and subselects may be nested:
.B "select src.name from (select path from /projects depth 2) as src where src.size > 1024"
.PP
A subquery may have its own
.BR where ", " "group by" ", " having ", " "order by" ", " limit " and " offset
clauses, but no
.BR into .
.PP
To reference the outer query's entry from a subquery, bind the outer search root with an alias using the
.B as
keyword; the inner query may then read the outer entry's fields and column aliases as
.IB ALIAS . NAME \fR:
.PP
.RS
.EX
fselect "name, path, size from /data as data
    where exists (select * from /backup as backup
    where backup.name = data.name)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
An alias declared inside a subquery is visible only there, so inner and outer queries may reuse a name.
In a grouped query a correlated subquery may read only the grouping columns and aggregate aliases of the outer row.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
Specified with the
.B into
keyword at the end of the query.
.TP
.B tabs
Tab-separated columns (default).
.TP
.B lines
Each column on a separate line.
.TP
.B list
Columns separated by a NUL byte, similar to
.B find \-print0
(suitable for
.BR "xargs \-0" ).
.TP
.B csv
Comma-separated values.
.TP
.B json
JSON array of result objects.
.TP
.B html
HTML document containing a table.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
On Linux:
.RS
.B ~/.config/fselect/config.toml
.RE
.PP
On Windows:
.RS
.B %APPDATA%\ejhspetersson\efselect\econfig.toml
.RE
.PP
If no config file is found on the standard paths,
.B fselect
also looks for a config file next to the executable.
A custom path may be supplied with
.BR \-\-config .
A fresh file with the defaults is created when none exists.
.PP
Recognised keys:
.TP
.BR no_color ", " us_dates ", " everything ", " plocate ", " check_for_updates
Booleans with the same meaning as the corresponding command-line options; the update check also needs the
.B update\-notifications
build feature.
.TP
.BR gitignore ", " hgignore ", " dockerignore
Booleans setting the default ignore-file handling for every search root.
.TP
.B default_file_size_format
Format modifier applied by the
.B fsize
column (see
.BR FORMAT_SIZE ).
.TP
.BR is_zip_archive ", " is_archive ", " is_audio ", " is_book ", " is_doc ", " is_font ", " is_image ", " is_source ", " is_video
Lists of file extensions behind the corresponding type-test fields.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
Start with
.BR "fselect \-i" .
In interactive mode:
.IP \(bu
Queries are entered directly without quoting for the shell.
.IP \(bu
Shell metacharacters need not be escaped.
.IP \(bu
Command history is available via the up and down arrow keys.
.IP \(bu
.B pwd
prints the current directory;
.B cd
changes it.
.IP \(bu
.B "errors off"
suppresses error reporting.
.IP \(bu
Exit with
.BR quit ,
.BR exit ,
Ctrl+C, or Ctrl+D.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LS_COLORS
Determines how search results are colorized; see
.BR dircolors (1).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set (to any value), disables all colorized output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
I/O error during directory traversal or file reading.
.TP
.B 2
Query parse error, or a fatal error while evaluating the query
(e.g. an invalid root alias).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Find temporary and config files, showing size and full path:
$ fselect size, path from /home/user where name = '*.cfg' or name = '*.tmp'
.TP
Search in a directory whose name contains spaces:
$ fselect "name from '/home/user/dir with spaces' where size > 0"
.TP
Find large files (over 2 GiB):
$ fselect size, abspath from ./tmp where size gt 2g
.TP
Find files within a size range:
$ fselect name, size from ./tmp where size between 5mb and 6mb
.TP
Complex boolean condition:
$ fselect "name from /tmp where (name = *.tmp and size = 0) or (name = *.cfg and size > 1000000)"
.TP
Find files modified today, ordered by size:
$ fselect path, size from /home/user where modified = today order by size desc
.TP
Find files by regular expression:
$ fselect name from /home/user where path =~ '.*Rust.*'
.TP
Aggregate: total size of downloads:
$ fselect "MIN(size), MAX(size), AVG(size), SUM(size), COUNT(*) from /home/user/Downloads"
.TP
Group files by extension with count:
$ fselect "ext, count(*) from /tmp group by ext"
.TP
Filter groups after aggregation:
$ fselect "ext, count(*) from /tmp group by ext having count(*) > 10"
.TP
Search with depth limit and symlink following:
$ fselect path from /home/user depth 5 symlinks where name = '*.jpg'
.TP
Search respecting .gitignore:
$ fselect size, path from /home/user/projects gitignore where name = '*.cpp'
.TP
Find images by dimensions:
$ fselect path from /home/user/photos where width gte 2000 or height gte 2000
.TP
Find old rap MP3s:
$ fselect duration, path from /home/user/music where genre = Rap and bitrate = 320 and mp3_year lt 2000
.TP
Find files with dangerous permissions:
$ fselect mode, path from /home/user where other_write or other_exec
.TP
Find files not present in a backup directory:
$ fselect "name, path from /current where name not in (select name from /backup)"
.TP
Return the five largest files:
$ fselect top 5 name, size from /home/user order by size desc
.TP
Name a column and reuse it:
$ fselect "size / 1024 as kb, name from /tmp where kb > 100 order by kb desc"
.TP
Compare with a value computed by a subquery:
$ fselect "name, size from /data where size > (select avg(size) from /data)"
.TP
Search the rows of an inner query:
$ fselect "src.name from (select path from /projects depth 2 where size > 100) as src where src.name like '%.rs'"
.TP
Output as JSON:
$ fselect size, path from /home/user limit 5 into json
.TP
Pipe NUL-separated paths to grep:
$ fselect path from /home/user into list | xargs -0 grep foobar
